From: Mirdispo contributors
Subject: Mend a measurement that could not mean what it said

Four things, all found by reading back over what was built in a hurry.

A line claimed to say how many frames carried a picture and how many only
said that nothing had changed, deciding by their size. That holds where an
encoder spends what a frame needs. It does not hold at all where the encoder
is told to hold a rate, because a held rate is held by padding a frame that
needed nothing up to the size of one that needed everything. Every frame
measured the same, and the line reported that all of them carried a picture
whether or not any of them did. It was believed for an evening. The sizes are
reported without the claim, and the smallest frame in each ten seconds is
given, which is where the question can actually be looked at.

How far behind a frame may fall before later ones are dropped was written in
milliseconds. The same milliseconds are a different number of frames at a
different rate, so the rule quietly tightened or loosened as the rate changed,
which nobody chose. It is counted in frames and turned into time once the
rate is known.

One encoder was given the same setting twice in the same call, with different
values. The second won and the first was never doing anything, which is only
a problem for whoever reads the file and believes the first one.

And the numbers the software encoder is held to were left unexplained, which
made them look stale rather than specific. They were measured on a group
sharing the lower band with the station connection on one radio: four
megabits dropped nothing but left the picture thin, six held two minutes,
eight came apart inside one, and five is where it settled. That is written
down now, because a machine with no upper band to move to has no other way to
find it.

